; A built-in predicate above its arity.
;
; `A` and `B` are distinct, so a reader of this rule expects it not to fire.
; Read at two arguments, `(eq ?x A B)` would drop the third in silence and
; evaluate a three-way equality as a two-way one, `(eq ?x A)` — a wrong
; answer with a success exit code.
;
; The grammar refuses it at the parse, at the third argument: `eq` compares
; exactly two values (Q-M1e.18), and a shape that stops being the
; primitive's is refused at the token where it stops. The program does not
; load.
(relation p T)
(relation q T)

(rule three-way-equality ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (eq ?x A B))
  :assert (q ?x))
